Code statements:
```java
@Override
protected void onDestroy() {
    super.onDestroy();
    DownloadManagerInterface.getInstance().destroy(this);
}
```

Code statements:
```java
@Override
protected void onDestroy() {
    super.onDestroy();
    DownloadManagerInterface.getInstance().destroy(this);
}
```

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
DownloadManagerInterface.getInstance().registerReceiver(downloadCompletionReceiver);
```
```java
// No unregister call in onDestroy
```
Reason:
The `DownloadManagerInterface` was bound to the `MainActivity`, and the absence of an `unregisterReceiver` call in the `onDestroy` method meant that the `downloadCompletionReceiver` remained active even after the activity was destroyed. This oversight could lead to memory leaks and unintended behavior since the receiver would still be listening for events that are no longer relevant.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
@Override
protected void onDestroy() {
    super.onDestroy();
    DownloadManagerInterface.getInstance().destroy(this);
}
```
Reason:
The addition of the `destroy(this)` method call in the `onDestroy` method ensures that the `DownloadManagerInterface` properly unregisters the `downloadCompletionReceiver` when the `MainActivity` is destroyed. This prevents memory leaks and ensures that the receiver is no longer active, thus maintaining the integrity of the application's lifecycle.

